  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Opening the market for Egyptian peanut kernels</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The applicant is Katjang Jerry B.V., who owns a peanut roasting and coating factory in St. Maartensdijk (Zeeland, the Netherlands). Local partner is Grow Tech for Agricultural Development in Cairo (Egypt), a producer and exporter of fresh agricultural produce (e.g. peanut, onions and potatoes).The project focuses on raw kernels for Egyptian snack producers and export. In Egypt mostly groundnuts of &apos;Virginia&apos;-type are grown. These are mainly exported in-shell. This industry has 40% rejected shelled nuts. To give more value to these rejected groundnuts, the project wants to start a shelling factory. Apart from shelling these rejects, the main purpose of the factory however will be the shelling of a different type of groundnut, the &apos;Spanish&apos;-type. This is a more round nut, preferred by the snack industry (borrelnootjes etc).To supply this industry, the groundnuts must be shelled, calibrated and packed in an appropriate way. These techniques don&apos;t yet exist in Egypt. The project works with its own farm and 25-50 outgrowers in the south of Egypt in an irrigation scheme. The circumstances are very suitable for aflatoxine-free production, which is very important as it is the main food-safety risk in groundnuts.The factory will provide labour for 100 workers. The project will in this way open the export market for groundnut kernels.</narrative>
  </description>
